# KNOWN GAP — assigning to the VALUE binding of a dict loop is ACCEPTED and
# SILENTLY DISCARDED. Core #10 silent drop, rc 0, no diagnostic.
#
#     for k, v in d:  v = 99      -> accepted, rc 0, d is UNCHANGED
#     for k, v in &d: v = 99      -> accepted, rc 0, d is UNCHANGED
#
# ⚠ THE VALUE POSITION IS SAFE TO WRITE THROUGH -- this is not a hazard being
# correctly refused, it is a write being swallowed. Three independent reasons
# the write is sound:
#   1. A dict's hash is computed from the KEY alone. The value is not part of
#      the bucket index, so changing it cannot misplace the entry.
#   2. In-place value mutation neither resizes nor rehashes, so there is no
#      realloc and therefore no iterator invalidation.
#   3. The only implementation obligation is DROPPING THE OLD VALUE when the
#      new one is installed.
#
# CONTRAST -- the KEY positions genuinely are hazardous, and must NOT be
# conflated with this one:
#   * `for x in &set`  -- the element IS the hash key.
#   * `for k in &dict` -- the binding IS the hash key.
#   Writing through either corrupts the bucket index. Their correct answer is
#   reject, or mutable-private-copy per the bare-form ruling
#   (`docs/define-gorget/decisions.md:2584-2578`, owner 2026-08-18).
#
# RELATED, and measured alongside: the natural in-loop update spelling
# `for k in d: d[k] = 99` dies at **build rc 101 with `E_CONSUME_SITES`** --
# an internal validator label, not a user diagnostic (filed separately).
# So today there is NO working in-loop dict-value update idiom; a user must
# snapshot the keys first (`Vector[String] ks = d.keys()`), which DOES work.
#
# UNRATIFIED, and deliberately not guessed here: whether `v = ...` in
# `for k, v in &d` is the sanctioned spelling, or whether users must write
# `d[k] = v`. That is a surface-taste call for the owner. EITHER WAY the
# current behaviour is a defect: accepting a write and discarding it is the
# worst of the three available outcomes.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 99.
# Measured 2026-08-27 at HEAD; orchestrator-verified, both spellings.

void main():
    Dict[String, int] d = {"a": 1, "b": 2}
    for k, v in &d:
        v = 99
    print(d["a"])
